.TH PSMatrixProductVM 3 2025-01-20 "PsyC 0.9.5" "PsyC Library Functions Manual"

.SH NAME
\fBPSMatrixProductVM\fR \- vector\-matrix multiplication

.SH LIBRARY
PsyC (-lpsyc)

.SH SYNOPSIS

.B
#include <psyc/maths.h>

.I
int  
.br
\fBPSMatrixProductVM\fR(\fIPSFloat *a\fR, \fIPSMatrix b\fR, \fIlong len\fR, \fIPSMatrix *result\fR, \fIPSMathOpts *opts\fR)
.SH DESCRIPTION
Performs vector-matrix multiplication between vector \fIa\fR and matrix \fIb\fR.
.br
Argument \fIlen\fR must be the length of the vector \fIa\fR.
.br
Results are stored into matrix pointed by \fIresult\fR. If pointer pointed by \fIresult\fR is \fINULL\fR, a new matrix is automatically allocated by the function itself and its pointer will be stored into \fIresult\fR.
.br
The function uses BLAS to compute the result. Anyway, if BLAS support is missing in PsyC build, function will compute results by using \fIPSDotProduct\fR as fallback.
.br
The acceleration method can be changed via the \fIacceleration\fR member of the optional \fIopt\fR argument.
.br
Matrix \fIb\fR can be transposed by using the  \fItranspose\fR field in the \fIopt\fR argument. In that case, \fItranspose\fR will contain the (1-based) indices of the operand arguments you want to be transposed:
.br

.RS 4
\[bu] opt->transpose = 2 (transpose matrix \fIb\fR)
.RE
.RE

By default, data in result vector will be overwritten. Anyway, if \fIPS_STORE_MODE_ADD\fR is set as \fIstore_mode\fR into \fIopts\fR, result will be added to data already present in the result vector.  

.SH RETURN VALUES
1 if operation succeeds, 0 if it fails.
Possible failure reasons:
.br

.RS 4
\[bu] \fIresult\fR is \fINULL\fR or \fIa\fR is \fINULL\fR or \fIb\fR is \fINULL\fR.
.br

\[bu] \fIa\fR has zero dimensions or \fIb\fR has zero dimensions.
.br

\[bu] Matrix aligment error.
.br

\[bu] Invalid result shape.
.br

\[bu] Matrix pointed by \fIresult\fR is not \fINULL\fR and its shape differs from resulting output shape.
.br

\[bu] Memory allocation failure.
.br
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
psyc(1), PSDotProduct(3), PSMatrixProduct(3), PSMatrixProductMV(3)
